  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Font & Theme**\n\nI\'m currently focused on incorporating custom fonts and theme colors. I considered adding these directly to `@theme` in `src/index.css`. Now, I\'m thinking of importing fonts like \"Inter\" or \"Space Grotesk,\" or declaring custom Tailwind variables. Ultimately, I\'ll need to edit `src/index.css` to implement these changes.\n\n\n**Defining Theme and Structure**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in designing the application\'s structure. I\'ve decided on some core colors: `--color-western-purple`, `--color-western-purple-light`, `--color-western-gold`, and `--color-western-gold-dark`. I will also use `Inter` as the default font. To organize this, I\'m creating `/src/types.ts` to outline the profile and scholarship models, and I am starting with the `StudentProfile` interface.\n\n\n**Defining Scholarship Structure**\n\nI am now structuring the `Scholarship` interface. It will include `id`, `name`, `organization`, `amount`, and `deadline`. I\'m also considering `isExpired`, `eligibilitySummary`, and `applicationUrl`. Furthermore, I\'ll incorporate scoring metadata: `targetDegree`, `targetFaculties`, `minGpaRange`, `targetCitizenships`, `targetProvinces`, `requiresFinancialNeed`, `targetIdentities`, and `targetInterests`.\n\n\n**Defining Scoring Rules**\n\nI am now delving into the scholarship scoring system and updating how the matching logic will operate. Degree level matching gets +20, implemented with \"Undergraduate\" (1st-4th year) and \"Graduate\" types. Location matches are also worth +20. I\'m carefully reviewing hard exclusions such as degree level mismatches to ensure these rules are correctly implemented.\n\n\n**Refining Province Matching**\n\nI\'m now refining the province-matching logic for scholarship eligibility. My goal is to ensure that matching is clear and comprehensive. I\'ve considered specific scenarios for Ontario residents, \"Outside Canada,\" and national scholarships. I am defining specific `targetProvinces` values, such as \"Ontario,\" \"Outside Canada,\" \"All,\" and \"Canadian Provinces,\" to aid my matching logic. I plan to define a precise scoring rule, ensuring that province matches are handled transparently, adding +20 when appropriate.\n\n\n**Defining Scoring Rules**\n\nI am now structuring the scholarship scoring system and working on refining its matching logic. Field of study matches are worth +15 if the student\'s faculty is within the scholarship\'s `targetFaculties`, including \"All.\" GPA requirements add +15 if the student\'s GPA range rank is at or above the scholarship\'s `minGpaRange` rank. If the scholarship doesn\'t have a minimum GPA, the requirement is considered met. Financial need matching adds +10 if the student\'s response aligns with the scholarship\'s `requiresFinancialNeed` status.\n\n\n**Defining Identity Matches**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the identity matching logic within the scoring system, which is +10 if a match is detected. If `targetIdentities` are present, I give +10 if any of them align with the student\'s selections. When the `targetIdentities` array is empty or includes \"All\" or \"None/prefer not to say\", any student qualifies, so +10 is applied. I\'m focusing on ensuring these rules are precisely implemented, especially the hard exclusions like expired deadlines and degree mismatches.\n\n\n\n**Calculating Interest Scores**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the activities and interests matching logic, now giving +10 if the student\'s selections align with any of the scholarship\'s `targetInterests`. If the scholarship\'s `targetInterests` is empty or includes \"All\", any student qualifies. After review, I\'ve calculated that the maximum possible scholarship score is a perfect 100: degree (20) + location (20) + faculty (15) + GPA (15) + financial need (10) + identity (10) + activities/interests (10). It\'s very intuitive! I plan to create a scholarship database soon.\n\n\n**Defining Scholarship Examples**\n\nI\'m now defining some specific scholarship examples to test my scoring system. I\'m focusing on attributes like degree, faculty, GPA, location, and interests for each one. I\'m setting values and details, ensuring I\'ve accounted for various scenarios and match types, for scholarships like the \"Western Undergraduate Scholarship\" or the \"Ontario Graduate Scholarship.\"\n\n\n**Defining Scholarship Examples**\n\nI\'m now detailing scholarship examples to test scoring. I\'ve defined the Western Indigenous Scholarship: Faculty: All, Identity: Indigenous, Financial Need: Yes, Value: $6,000, Degree: Undergraduate or Graduate. I\'ve also added the Western Black Student Scholarship. It\'s Degree: Undergraduate or Graduate, Faculty: All, Identity: Black student, GPA: 75\342\200\22379%+, Value: $5,000.\n\n\n**Adding Scholarship Examples**\n\nI\'m now detailing scholarship examples to test and validate the scoring and matching system. I have just added the Western STEM Excellence Scholarship, targeted toward an undergraduate science or engineering student with 90%+ GPA, leadership/STEM research interests, and a substantial value of $100,000 - $120,000. I also added the Western International Student Award, which has all degrees, all faculties, and targeted toward International Students. I have also added Out-of-Province Leadership, Western Disabilities Award, LGBTQ2S+ Leader Scholarship, and a First-Gen Business Trailblazer Award.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 21
      nanos: 9535109
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
