  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Scholarship Data**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got the JSON data from the user and it\'s looking good! Now, I\'m diving into `src/data/scholarships.ts` to figure out the right attributes to map these fields. The user data contains `name` and `organization` fields; I\'m working to fully integrate the attributes!\n\n\n**Mapping Scholarship Attributes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on mapping the user\'s data to specific attributes. I\'m prioritizing the inclusion of `amount`, `deadline`, `degree_level`, `field_of_study`, `location`, `eligibility_summary`, and `tags` within the TypeScript interface. I\'m also considering including `application_url` and `notes`.\n\n\n**Structuring Scholarship Format**\n\nI\'m now working to translate the user\'s scholarship data into the `Scholarship` interface. My focus is on ensuring a smooth fit with the existing rule-based matching logic, specifically examining the `targetDegree`, `targetFaculties`, `minGpaRange`, and other rule attributes. I\'m focusing on ensuring data compatibility, especially with the `target` attributes. I\'m aiming for a clean and efficient translation.\n\n\n**Evaluating Degree Level**\n\nI\'ve got the degree level matching figured out! I\'ve formalized the undergraduate and graduate checks and simplified things by making `targetDegree` as \"All\", \"Undergraduate\", or \"Graduate\". I\'m making sure it aligns with student type (`isStudentUndergrad` and `isStudentGrad`).  I\'ve also realized that details like \"entering 1st year\" don\'t affect the initial degree-level match. If there is a degree-level mismatch, I now correctly ensure hard exclusions.\n\n\n**Refining Target Degree Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the degree matching logic based on specific examples. I\'ve considered that scholarships specifying \"Undergraduate (4th year), Graduate\" or \"Graduate (Master\'s or professional undergraduate)\" should both be categorized as `targetDegree: \'All\'`. This ensures inclusivity for both undergraduate and graduate student types. I\'m also reviewing how the matcher handles province and citizenship; specifically the `isProvinceMatch` and `isCitizenshipMatch` logic within the `matcher.ts` file.\n\n\n**Determining Scholarship Location**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `isProvinceMatch` and `isCitizenshipMatch` logic within the `matcher.ts` file, and assessing how the `locationPoints` are assigned. If the scholarship location is \"Canada\", I\'m thinking of assigning the `targetProvinces` as \"All\", and `targetCitizenships` as \"Canadian citizen, Permanent resident, Protected/refugee status\". This should ensure an accurate location match. I\'m focusing on ensuring data compatibility with a range of location scenarios.\n\n\n**Categorizing Faculty Data**\n\nI\'m now determining how to categorize faculties accurately. I\'ve compiled a mapping for faculties. For example, \"Advertising, Marketing\" translates to `[\'Business/Ivey\', \'Other\']`, and \"Medicine\" is categorized as `[\'Health Sciences\']`. I\'m evaluating the mapping for various faculty combinations to ensure robust categorizations for the `targetFaculties` field, like mapping \"Social Science\" to `[\'Social Science\']`. I\'m also ensuring a range of general categories, and also \"All\" when relevant.\n\n\n**Defining Scholarship Requirements**\n\nI\'m now detailing the rules for scholarship matching. I\'ll automatically include a `minGpaRange` if the value isn\'t specified, and adjust it based on the required average (70-74%, 75-79%, 80-84%, 85-89%, or 90%+). I\'ll also set `requiresFinancialNeed` to `true` if the `financial_need` tag exists. Plus, I\'ll map identity tags like `disability`, `Indigenous`, `Black_student`, `women`, or `LGBTQ2S+` to their corresponding values.\n\n\n"
